1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,591
Many SAT statistics questions
don't ask you to calculate much.

2
00:00:04,611 --> 00:00:07,912
They ask whether you understand
what the numbers mean.

3
00:00:07,932 --> 00:00:09,948
What an outlier does to the mean.

4
00:00:09,968 --> 00:00:11,555
Which data set is more spread out.

5
00:00:11,575 --> 00:00:13,520
What a margin of error tells you.

6
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:16,468
And what a study can and can't prove.

7
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,963
The most common mistake is overclaiming.

8
00:00:18,983 --> 00:00:21,821
Saying a survey proves
something about everyone,

9
00:00:21,841 --> 00:00:24,984
or that a study proves cause
and effect when it can't.

10
00:00:25,234 --> 00:00:26,820
We'll build a simple framework for that,

11
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:30,590
and work through six practice questions
with made-up data along the way.

12
00:00:30,626 --> 00:00:31,856
Start with center.

13
00:00:31,876 --> 00:00:36,427
The mean is the average: add everything
up and divide by how many values there

14
00:00:36,447 --> 00:00:37,106
are.

15
00:00:37,126 --> 00:00:40,498
The median is the middle value
when the data is in order.

16
00:00:40,518 --> 00:00:44,376
With an even number of values, it's
the average of the two middle ones.

17
00:00:44,427 --> 00:00:47,228
An outlier is a value far from the rest.

18
00:00:47,248 --> 00:00:50,049
Outliers pull the mean toward them, a lot.

19
00:00:50,069 --> 00:00:53,657
The median barely moves, because
it only cares about position,

20
00:00:53,677 --> 00:00:54,978
not size.

21
00:00:54,998 --> 00:00:59,335
Change that eight to eighty, and the
mean jumps from four to eighteen point

22
00:00:59,355 --> 00:00:59,907
four.

23
00:00:59,927 --> 00:01:01,852
The median is still three.

24
00:01:01,872 --> 00:01:06,280
That's why, when there's an outlier,
the median usually describes a typical

25
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:08,407
value better than the mean.

26
00:01:08,455 --> 00:01:09,633
Now spread.

27
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:12,720
The range is the maximum
minus the minimum.

28
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,882
It's quick, but it only
looks at the two ends.

29
00:01:15,902 --> 00:01:20,060
Standard deviation measures how far
the values typically are from the mean.

30
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,810
Data bunched close to the mean
has a small standard deviation.

31
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:26,794
Data spread out toward
the ends has a large one.

32
00:01:26,829 --> 00:01:30,417
On the SAT, you'll usually
compare standard deviations,

33
00:01:30,437 --> 00:01:31,830
not calculate them.

34
00:01:31,872 --> 00:01:33,372
Next, samples.

35
00:01:33,586 --> 00:01:36,995
When a survey picks people
at random from a population,

36
00:01:37,015 --> 00:01:40,622
the sample result is an estimate
for the whole population.

37
00:01:40,765 --> 00:01:45,479
The margin of error turns that estimate
into a range of plausible values.

38
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:48,132
Say a random sample shows forty percent,

39
00:01:48,203 --> 00:01:50,989
with a margin of error of
three percentage points.

40
00:01:51,061 --> 00:01:55,647
Then it's plausible that between
thirty-seven and forty-three percent of the

41
00:01:55,667 --> 00:01:57,755
whole population agrees.

42
00:01:57,775 --> 00:01:59,811
Plausible, not guaranteed.

43
00:01:59,953 --> 00:02:04,087
And larger random samples generally
give smaller margins of error.

44
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:06,622
Here's what a margin of
error does not tell you.

45
00:02:06,642 --> 00:02:09,159
It isn't a range for individual people.

46
00:02:09,179 --> 00:02:12,230
It doesn't apply to a
population that wasn't sampled.

47
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:16,607
And it can't fix a sample that
wasn't random in the first place.

48
00:02:16,672 --> 00:02:18,493
Last, study design.

49
00:02:18,742 --> 00:02:20,079
Ask two questions.

50
00:02:20,099 --> 00:02:23,009
Was the sample chosen at
random from a population?

51
00:02:23,029 --> 00:02:25,636
If yes, you can generalize
to that population.

52
00:02:25,742 --> 00:02:28,188
Were subjects randomly
assigned to treatments?

53
00:02:28,208 --> 00:02:31,808
If yes, it's an experiment, and you
can talk about cause and effect.

54
00:02:31,828 --> 00:02:37,701
If no one was assigned anything, and
researchers just observed what people

55
00:02:37,721 --> 00:02:40,828
already do, it's an observational study.

56
00:02:41,006 --> 00:02:45,042
That can show an association,
but not cause and effect.

57
00:02:45,062 --> 00:02:47,113
So when a statistics question comes up,

58
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:48,398
sort it first.

59
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:49,078
Center?

60
00:02:49,098 --> 00:02:50,328
Look for an outlier.

61
00:02:50,348 --> 00:02:51,292
Spread?

62
00:02:51,312 --> 00:02:54,184
Compare how far the data
sits from the middle.

63
00:02:54,204 --> 00:02:56,435
A value added or groups combined?

64
00:02:56,455 --> 00:02:57,613
Use totals.

65
00:02:57,633 --> 00:02:58,542
A survey?

66
00:02:58,562 --> 00:02:59,864
Read the margin of error.

67
00:02:59,884 --> 00:03:00,756
A study?

68
00:03:00,776 --> 00:03:02,669
Check sampling and assignment.

69
00:03:02,709 --> 00:03:03,546
Question one.

70
00:03:03,566 --> 00:03:07,617
The list shows the number of books each
of eight students read over the summer:

71
00:03:07,637 --> 00:03:11,831
three, four, four, five, five, six, seven,

72
00:03:11,851 --> 00:03:12,851
and thirty.

73
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:15,402
If the value thirty is
removed from the list,

74
00:03:15,422 --> 00:03:16,942
which statement is true?

75
00:03:16,962 --> 00:03:18,248
Pause and try it.

76
00:03:21,469 --> 00:03:25,576
Thirty is the outlier, so removing
it will pull the mean down a lot.

77
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:27,040
Now the median.

78
00:03:27,183 --> 00:03:30,842
With eight values, it's the average
of the fourth and fifth values:

79
00:03:30,862 --> 00:03:32,806
five and five, so five.

80
00:03:32,826 --> 00:03:35,127
Without thirty, there are seven values,

81
00:03:35,147 --> 00:03:37,755
and the middle one, the
fourth, is still five.

82
00:03:37,805 --> 00:03:40,142
The mean goes from sixty-four over eight,

83
00:03:40,162 --> 00:03:42,607
which is eight, to thirty-four over seven,

84
00:03:42,627 --> 00:03:44,178
about four point nine.

85
00:03:44,198 --> 00:03:47,428
So the mean decreases and
the median stays the same.

86
00:03:47,448 --> 00:03:48,948
The answer is C.

87
00:03:49,055 --> 00:03:53,269
In Desmos, the mean and median
functions on a list will confirm both.

88
00:03:53,292 --> 00:03:54,308
Question two.

89
00:03:54,328 --> 00:03:59,042
In class A, the mean score of twenty
students on a test was seventy-eight.

90
00:03:59,435 --> 00:04:10,007
In class B, the mean score of thirty
students on the same test was eighty-eight.

91
00:04:10,471 --> 00:04:13,542
What is the mean score
of all fifty students?

92
00:04:13,575 --> 00:04:14,861
Pause and try it.

93
00:04:19,082 --> 00:04:21,026
Don't average the two means.

94
00:04:21,046 --> 00:04:23,740
Class B has more students,
so it counts more.

95
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:24,883
Switch to totals.

96
00:04:24,903 --> 00:04:27,419
Twenty times seventy-eight
is fifteen sixty.

97
00:04:27,439 --> 00:04:30,132
Thirty times eighty-eight
is twenty-six forty.

98
00:04:30,152 --> 00:04:32,618
Together, that's forty-two hundred points.

99
00:04:32,642 --> 00:04:37,607
Divide by all fifty students: forty-two
hundred over fifty is eighty-four.

100
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,392
The trap is eighty-three, the average
of seventy-eight and eighty-eight.

101
00:04:42,642 --> 00:04:45,479
Sense check: eighty-four
is closer to eighty-eight,

102
00:04:45,499 --> 00:04:47,857
because the bigger class
had the higher mean.

103
00:04:47,893 --> 00:04:49,551
Question three.

104
00:04:49,571 --> 00:04:52,857
Two data sets each have nine values.

105
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,264
Data set P is one, one, one, one, five,

106
00:04:56,284 --> 00:04:57,907
nine, nine, nine, nine.

107
00:04:57,927 --> 00:05:00,908
Data set Q is one, four, five, five, five,

108
00:05:00,928 --> 00:05:02,658
five, five, six, nine.

109
00:05:02,678 --> 00:05:06,678
Which statement about the standard
deviations of the two data sets is true?

110
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:07,985
Pause and try it.

111
00:05:12,206 --> 00:05:13,206
Don't calculate.

112
00:05:13,278 --> 00:05:13,829
Look.

113
00:05:13,849 --> 00:05:16,865
Both sets have a mean of
five and a range of eight,

114
00:05:16,885 --> 00:05:18,936
so the range can't tell them apart.

115
00:05:18,956 --> 00:05:22,115
But in P, almost every
value sits at the ends,

116
00:05:22,135 --> 00:05:23,579
four away from the mean.

117
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:26,849
In Q, most values sit right on the mean.

118
00:05:26,876 --> 00:05:31,463
Values farther from the mean
means a larger standard deviation.

119
00:05:31,483 --> 00:05:33,070
So P's is greater.

120
00:05:33,090 --> 00:05:34,447
The answer is A.

121
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:38,534
If you want proof, Desmos has
a standard deviation function:

122
00:05:38,554 --> 00:05:41,652
P comes out to four, and Q is about two.

123
00:05:41,672 --> 00:05:46,223
C is the trap for anyone who thinks
equal ranges mean equal spread.

124
00:05:46,243 --> 00:05:48,937
Range only sees the two ends.

125
00:05:48,957 --> 00:05:51,421
Standard deviation sees every value.

126
00:05:51,477 --> 00:05:52,672
Question four.

127
00:05:52,692 --> 00:05:57,065
A researcher selected four hundred
residents of a city at random and asked

128
00:05:57,085 --> 00:05:59,513
whether they support building a new park.

129
00:05:59,549 --> 00:06:02,835
Of those surveyed,
sixty-two percent said yes.

130
00:06:02,906 --> 00:06:07,100
The estimate has an associated margin
of error of five percentage points.

131
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:09,477
Which conclusion is most appropriate?

132
00:06:09,549 --> 00:06:10,834
Pause and try it.

133
00:06:15,055 --> 00:06:19,500
Sixty-two, plus or minus five, gives a
range from fifty-seven to sixty-seven

134
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:20,520
percent.

135
00:06:20,698 --> 00:06:24,999
That range is a plausible estimate for
the population the sample came from:

136
00:06:25,019 --> 00:06:26,805
residents of this city.

137
00:06:27,090 --> 00:06:28,663
The answer is D.

138
00:06:28,698 --> 00:06:29,357
A is too certain.

139
00:06:29,377 --> 00:06:33,714
A sample gives an estimate,
not an exact value.

140
00:06:33,734 --> 00:06:37,141
B assumes a second random sample
would come out exactly the same,

141
00:06:37,161 --> 00:06:39,357
but every random sample varies a little.

142
00:06:39,377 --> 00:06:43,269
And C jumps to the whole state,
a population nobody sampled.

143
00:06:43,291 --> 00:06:44,664
Question five.

144
00:06:44,684 --> 00:06:48,450
A researcher selected six hundred
adults at random from a large city and

145
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:49,521
surveyed them.

146
00:06:49,541 --> 00:06:54,093
Adults who reported eating breakfast
every day tended to report higher energy

147
00:06:54,113 --> 00:06:56,148
levels than adults who did not.

148
00:06:56,362 --> 00:06:58,648
Which conclusion is best supported?

149
00:06:58,717 --> 00:07:00,003
Pause and try it.

150
00:07:04,224 --> 00:07:05,509
Run the two checks.

151
00:07:05,724 --> 00:07:07,061
Random sampling?

152
00:07:07,081 --> 00:07:12,474
Yes, from this city, so the result can
be generalized to adults in this city.

153
00:07:12,902 --> 00:07:14,081
Random assignment?

154
00:07:14,224 --> 00:07:14,704
No.

155
00:07:14,724 --> 00:07:16,418
Nobody was told to eat breakfast.

156
00:07:16,438 --> 00:07:18,188
People chose for themselves.

157
00:07:18,509 --> 00:07:21,704
That makes it observational,
so you only get an association.

158
00:07:21,724 --> 00:07:23,188
The answer is C.

159
00:07:23,215 --> 00:07:25,822
A and B both claim cause and effect.

160
00:07:26,001 --> 00:07:28,766
Maybe people who eat
breakfast also sleep more,

161
00:07:28,786 --> 00:07:30,195
or exercise more.

162
00:07:30,215 --> 00:07:32,945
An observational study
can't separate those.

163
00:07:32,965 --> 00:07:35,908
B also stretches to everyone, everywhere.

164
00:07:35,928 --> 00:07:40,659
And D is wrong because a random sample
of six hundred is plenty to say something

165
00:07:40,679 --> 00:07:41,706
about the city.

166
00:07:41,726 --> 00:07:43,777
Last one, and the hardest.

167
00:07:43,797 --> 00:07:48,778
A researcher recruited eighty student
volunteers from a large school district.

168
00:07:48,798 --> 00:07:54,099
The researcher randomly assigned forty
of them to study vocabulary with app A,

169
00:07:54,119 --> 00:07:56,349
and the other forty with app B.

170
00:07:56,369 --> 00:08:01,278
After six weeks, the students who used
app A had significantly higher mean

171
00:08:01,298 --> 00:08:03,455
scores on a vocabulary test.

172
00:08:03,475 --> 00:08:05,976
Which conclusion is best supported?

173
00:08:06,024 --> 00:08:07,111
Pause and try it.

174
00:08:07,131 --> 00:08:08,560
Take a little longer on this one.

175
00:08:12,766 --> 00:08:13,766
Two checks again.

176
00:08:13,837 --> 00:08:14,674
Random assignment?

177
00:08:14,694 --> 00:08:15,388
Yes.

178
00:08:15,408 --> 00:08:19,567
Chance decided who used which app, so
the two groups should be similar in every

179
00:08:19,587 --> 00:08:20,245
other way.

180
00:08:20,265 --> 00:08:22,817
That means the study can
support cause and effect.

181
00:08:22,837 --> 00:08:24,423
So C is out.

182
00:08:24,443 --> 00:08:27,551
This is an experiment, not
an observational study.

183
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:28,457
Random sampling?

184
00:08:28,477 --> 00:08:29,243
No.

185
00:08:29,263 --> 00:08:32,564
These are volunteers, not a
random sample of the district.

186
00:08:32,584 --> 00:08:35,099
Volunteers might be more
motivated than other students,

187
00:08:35,119 --> 00:08:37,492
so you can't generalize
to the whole district.

188
00:08:37,512 --> 00:08:39,048
A is out.

189
00:08:39,263 --> 00:08:42,011
But that doesn't erase the
result, so D is out too.

190
00:08:42,031 --> 00:08:46,245
The answer is B: cause and effect,
for students like the volunteers.

191
00:08:46,274 --> 00:08:49,076
This is the most common
mix-up in the whole topic.

192
00:08:49,096 --> 00:08:51,933
Random assignment
answers: does it cause it?

193
00:08:51,953 --> 00:08:54,503
Random sampling answers:
who does it apply to?

194
00:08:54,523 --> 00:08:57,988
They're separate questions,
and you need both answers.

195
00:08:58,057 --> 00:09:00,001
Here's the framework for test day.

196
00:09:00,021 --> 00:09:04,486
If there's an outlier, the mean
moves and the median mostly doesn't.

197
00:09:04,664 --> 00:09:09,251
If you're comparing spread, look at
how far the values sit from the mean,

198
00:09:09,271 --> 00:09:10,878
not just the range.

199
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:13,823
If values are added, removed, or combined,

200
00:09:13,843 --> 00:09:15,200
switch to totals.

201
00:09:15,236 --> 00:09:19,287
If there's a margin of error, give a
plausible range for the population that was

202
00:09:19,307 --> 00:09:20,037
sampled.

203
00:09:20,057 --> 00:09:22,680
Never an exact value, and
never a different group.

204
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:26,286
And for any study, ask two
things: random sampling,

205
00:09:26,306 --> 00:09:27,772
then random assignment.

206
00:09:27,814 --> 00:09:31,473
Every question in this video
was written for this video,

207
00:09:31,493 --> 00:09:34,936
with made-up data, in the
style of the real test.

208
00:09:34,956 --> 00:09:39,651
For official practice, the free Question
Bank lets you filter by skill under

209
00:09:39,671 --> 00:09:42,314
Problem-Solving and Data Analysis.

210
00:09:42,399 --> 00:09:45,271
Next up: how the adaptive
digital SAT works,

211
00:09:45,291 --> 00:09:46,772
module by module.

212
